\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Massive graph Green functions}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite vertex set, with positive edge weights,
and let #{L} be its \vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}.
For #{m>0}, define the \newvocab{massive precision} and its
\newvocab{Green operator} by
##{Q_m=L+m^2I,\qquad G_m=Q_m^{-1}.}
The inverse exists even when the graph is disconnected. Indeed, the
[energy identity](fdip-0008) gives, for #{f\ne0},
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle f,Q_mf\rangle_V
 &=\sum_e w(e)(Df)(e)^2\\
 &\quad+m^2\sum_v f(v)^2>0.
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{Q_mf=0} implies #{f=0}. Finite dimension and rank-nullity
make this injective endomorphism surjective, so it is an isomorphism.
The [inverse-response lemma](fdip-001Q) now gives a self-adjoint
inverse and the unique solution #{f=G_mj} to #{Q_mf=j}.
The endomorphism #{Q_m} is a
\vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004}; its
[counting array](fdip-002O) realizes it as a positive symmetric matrix. For #{j\ne0},
write #{x=G_mj\ne0}; then
#{\langle j,G_mj\rangle_V=\langle Q_mx,x\rangle_V>0}.
On an empty vertex set the same map argument gives the unique
isomorphism of zero modules; there are no point-source columns.}

\p{By the [real spectral theorem](fdip-002R), choose an orthonormal
eigenbasis #{u_a} of the self-adjoint operator #{L}, with eigenvalues #{\lambda_a\ge0}.
The energy identity proves this last inequality by evaluation on each
unit eigenvector. Put #{q_a=\lambda_a+m^2>0}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_m&=\sum_a q_a u_au_a^T,\\
G_m&=\sum_a q_a^{-1}u_au_a^T.
\end{aligned}}
Since #{u_a^Tu_b=\delta_{ab}} and #{\sum_a u_au_a^T=I}, multiplication
gives #{Q_mG_m=G_mQ_m=I}. Also #{G_m^T=G_m}, and
#{f^TG_mf=\sum_a q_a^{-1}(u_a^Tf)^2>0} for nonzero #{f}.}

\p{The \newvocab{Green kernel} is defined from the inverse map by
#{G_m(v,w)=(G_m\delta_w)(v)}. These are its matrix entries in the
counting coordinate basis. Its column at #{w}
is the unique field solving
##{Q_mG_m(\cdot,w)=\delta_w,}
where #{\delta_w(v)} is one at #{v=w} and zero elsewhere.
For a source field #{j}, the unique solution of #{Q_mf=j} is
#{f(v)=\sum_wG_m(v,w)j(w)}. No vertex-volume factor occurs because
the vertex pairing and basis are counting.}

\p{This convention has to be matched when comparing lattice Green
functions. Wingate's lattice action uses a positive operator built from
minus the backward-forward difference and a mass term
\citet{Section 4, Equations (4.2)-(4.5), p. 3}{wingate2025lattice}.
For unit spacing, that difference term has the sign of our #{L}.}

\p{In \citet{Theorem 8.26, p. 399}{friedli2017statistical}, the massive
lattice precision is
##{Q=-\frac{1}{2d}\Delta+m^2I.}
Here #{\Delta f(x)=\sum_{y\sim x}(f(y)-f(x))}. The factor #{1/(2d)}
and the lattice boundary conditions belong to that normalization.
Identifying their covariance with an inverse requires that precision,
not an unscaled adjacency Laplacian.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.PosDef.isUnit} proves invertibility of a
positive definite matrix, and \leanref{Matrix.PosDef.inv} proves
positivity of its inverse. These apply to the matrix step after the
graph-energy calculation supplies positivity. The kernel convention and
its graph interpretation are specified here.}
